[Music]  Emma is a latina hairdresser a stay at  home orders went into effect and large  parts of the economy shut down she is  among the tens of millions of workers  who have lost their jobs in just a  couple of months a closer look at data  show how that economic pain is  distributed among Americans and it turns  out that job losses during the pandemic  have fallen disproportionately on people  like Emma here's how gender occupation  and race data can help us understand  record unemployment numbers it's  actually unique to this unemployment  crisis that women have been the hardest  hit in previous recessions women tended  to fare better than men men held the  dominant share of jobs in sectors like  manufacturing and construction which  typically bear the brunt of an economic  downturn during the 2009 financial  crisis unemployment among men reached  11.1% compared to a peak of 9% for women  this period also marked the first time  in US history that women outnumbered men  in the workforce since many of them had  occupations and sectors that were more  resilient during a recession like  education or health care economists say  many married women who hadn't been  employed also joined the workforce to  replace or supplement their partners  lost income by 2019 women outnumbered  men in the u.s. paid workforce for the  second time in history  growth in service sector professions  actually allowed women to overtake men  as the dominant share of all non-farm  payrolls that occurred you know at the  end of last year but the pandemic has  now upended that women account for about  77 percent of workers and occupations  that require close personal contact and  can't easily be done remotely which is  you know a lot of those jobs have been  hit by the social distancing measures  and so those are jobs in food  preparation health care support and  personal service for instance the  unemployment rates for both black and  Hispanic women were higher than the  unemployment rate for white women if you  dive a bit deeper into the data you see  that the service sector jobs that can't  be done from home employ more black and  Latinas for many working from home is  not an option people are having to make  very difficult choices between what's  best for their economic well-being and  what's best for their health and public  safety Valerie Wilson is an economist  and director of the race and ethnicity  program at the Economic Policy Institute  she stresses that economic insecurity  isn't the only hurdle Hispanic and black  women face workers of color are in a  very difficult predicament based on  their position in the labor market and  in this economy and society as a whole  status showed that women tend to spend  more time than men with household  responsibilities and childcare making  job search is much harder on top of that  most black and Latinas who work in low  paid jobs are also the sole or primary  breadwinners in their households  according to a women's rights group  Wilson and other economists expect the  road to recovery to be even steeper for  black women and Latinas  I think getting back to work will be  harder for women and women of color in  particular because when we recover the  same jobs that were lost  probably  will not return as quickly so that will  be one factor in a slower recovery in  those communities  [Music]

